WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.118
(makes bird call)

00:00:01.118 --> 00:00:02.405
- Don't go anywhere.

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:04.682
- [Voiceover] Being in a
bald eagle nest is surreal.

00:00:04.682 --> 00:00:06.324
This is a wild animal.

00:00:06.324 --> 00:00:07.838
- She's being rowdy.

00:00:07.838 --> 00:00:09.546
- [Voiceover] It's very
capable of hurting you

00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:10.708
if you're not careful,

00:00:10.708 --> 00:00:12.521
so you have to take your time.

00:00:12.521 --> 00:00:14.619
- A little rough, huh? Sorry about that.

00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:18.786
- [Voiceover] It's an experience
that's hard to describe.

00:00:20.425 --> 00:00:22.060
- My name is Jim Campbell-Spickler.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.993
I'm a forest canopy ecologist

00:00:23.993 --> 00:00:26.410
and a wildlife biologist.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.577
And I'm pretty sure I have
the best job in the world.

00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:34.013
(birds tweeting)

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:37.194
- [Voiceover] My job is to
climb into the eagle's nest

00:00:37.194 --> 00:00:39.920
and capture and work with chicks.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.203
- Yeah, he's a monster.

00:00:41.203 --> 00:00:42.920
- [Voiceover] And the reason
why we're doing this is,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.385
we're trying to get blood samples.

00:00:45.385 --> 00:00:47.647
and we can use those blood samples

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.619
to look at environmental contaminants

00:00:50.619 --> 00:00:54.104
and how they get into the
bald eagles' food source.

00:00:54.104 --> 00:00:55.650
- Gonna make me chase ya, huh?

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.464
Why don't you come off there. Come down.

00:00:57.464 --> 00:01:00.002
- [Voiceover] It's a challenging
species to work with.

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:02.904
It's got a really sharp, hooked beak.

00:01:02.904 --> 00:01:05.966
And its talons are big and powerful.

00:01:05.966 --> 00:01:08.140
And you don't wanna let
him grab ahold of you,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.202
because they can really do some damage.

00:01:10.202 --> 00:01:12.285
- Alright. Let's go down.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:14.720
- [Voiceover] This is a wild animal

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.477
so you never know what could happen.

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.916
(gasps and groans)

00:01:20.916 --> 00:01:23.433
The Channel Islands are an island chain

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.470
off the coast of Southern California.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:26.832
- These two guys don't look too bad.

00:01:26.832 --> 00:01:28.789
- [Voiceover] They're
a desert island system.

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:31.452
You get out there and you can
stand on top of these bluffs

00:01:31.452 --> 00:01:33.190
and you can see the Pacific.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.698
A whole 360-degree view

00:01:35.698 --> 00:01:40.112
of just the most beautiful
ocean you can ever imagine.

00:01:40.112 --> 00:01:42.449
You've got not only bald eagles,

00:01:42.449 --> 00:01:44.209
you have peregrine falcons

00:01:44.209 --> 00:01:45.737
that are nesting on the sea cliffs.

00:01:45.737 --> 00:01:47.676
- Just tell me if she's comin' in.

00:01:47.676 --> 00:01:48.951
(grunts)

00:01:48.951 --> 00:01:52.719
- [Voiceover] You've got a
host of different sea birds.

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:55.219
And it's just a magical place.

00:01:57.222 --> 00:01:59.674
Our research, it may
look invasive, but really,

00:01:59.674 --> 00:02:03.313
one of our primary concerns is the safety

00:02:03.313 --> 00:02:05.657
of these young birds that we're handling,

00:02:05.657 --> 00:02:07.151
so we go to great lengths

00:02:07.151 --> 00:02:09.626
to ensure that we don't injure them

00:02:09.626 --> 00:02:12.240
and that when we put
them back in the nest,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.886
they're just as well-off

00:02:13.886 --> 00:02:16.335
as when we first encountered them.

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:17.360
- He likes you.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.683
- [Woman] Oh, hi!

00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:20.285
- The bald eagle's our national bird

00:02:20.285 --> 00:02:21.517
so it's quite an honor for me

00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:23.527
to get to work with this species.

00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:26.568
Quite often I'll just sit
in that nest for a moment

00:02:26.568 --> 00:02:27.870
and just take in the view

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.448
and just think, "wow," you know,

00:02:29.448 --> 00:02:31.335
It's just an honor to get up there,

00:02:31.335 --> 00:02:34.152
be able to share that view
and that experience with them

00:02:34.152 --> 00:02:36.569
just for that moment in time.

